{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqsiyvoyset","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"OVERRIDE bench embedded daemon code 1 is graceful teardown artifact not a mid drain crash","summary":"SPEC IS INCOMPLETE: prior notes (note-mqsexg8x2ff, note-mqsf07wpwcj) state \"the bench embedded daemon exits code=1 mid-run during the canonical-wiring judge-drain phase (embed sidecar logs 'no heartbeat 2min — daemon gone')\" and treat it as a daemon crash blocking the our-way-prod bench. That diagnosis is WRONG and sent two investigations down a dead end. Verified on Windows 11 from the worktree (HEAD 00806352): the embedded daemon does NOT crash during the drain. The \"[daemon.py] daemon pid=X exited with code=1\" line is printed by bench/zonoid_bench/daemon.py stop() at TEARDOWN, and on Windows proc.terminate() == TerminateProcess (a hard kill -> exit code 1). It only appears mid-run because daemon.py logs go to stderr and the bench's stdout/stderr interleave when piped. Proof the daemon is alive throughout the drain: spawned Popen pid == daemon.pid pidfile, GET /health returns phase:ready, and Popen.poll() stays None until stop() is called; only after stop() does returncode become 1. The embed sidecar logs \"no heartbeat 2min\" only when a daemon is left to idle WITHOUT a clean stop, not as a crash cause. Fix (commit c4ea7e02 on the attempt branch): daemon.py stop() now sends CTRL_BREAK_EVENT to the daemon's process group (started with CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP) for a GRACEFUL exit 0, falling back to terminate()/kill() only on timeout; daemon.js adds SIGBREAK to its SIGINT/SIGTERM graceful handler so CTRL_BREAK is honored. Teardown now reports \"stopped, code=0 [graceful]\" (verified across 3x start/stop cycles and a full limit-1 run). The SEPARATE, real reason our-way-prod sometimes answers \"I don't know\" is honest retrieval/answer variance: the only answer-bearing chunk (chunk-7, \"your dealership was able to resolve the issue with your GPS system\") is semantically distant from 